## Why This Is Asked

Tests distributed-systems thinking: exactly-once, dedup, backpressure, observability.

## Key Concepts

- Exactly-once processing
- Deduplication with dedup_id
- Backpressure strategies (batching, pause vs drop)
- Observability (tracing, metrics, logs)
- Kafka vs Kinesis trade-offs
- Idempotent sinks and compensating actions

## Code Example

```javascript
// Pseudocode for idempotent sink write
function writeEvent(evt, sink, store) {
  const id = evt.dedup_id;
  if (store.seen(id)) return;
  sink.write(evt);
  store.markSeen(id);
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you test guarantees for partial failures?
- How would you ensure exactly-once with at-least-once queues?

## Why This Is Asked

Test candidate's ability to design scalable security controls for multi-tenant K8s, covering RBAC, PodSecurity, image provenance, network isolation, and policy enforcement with Gatekeeper. Expect explanation of trade-offs (complexity vs. security) and how to validate in CI.

## Key Concepts

- Least privilege RBAC per-namespace
- Pod Security Standards
- OPA Gatekeeper constraint templates
- Image provenance and signed images
- NetworkPolicy segmentation
- Audit logging and policy testing

## Code Example

```javascript
// Test helper: ensures pods use allowed registries and non-root users
function isPodCompliant(pod){
  const okNamespace = pod.metadata.namespace.startsWith('team-');
  const okImage = pod.spec.containers.every(c => c.image.startsWith('registry.internal/'));
  const okUser = pod.spec.containers.every(c => c.securityContext?.runAsNonRoot === true);
  return okNamespace && okImage && okUser;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you test Gatekeeper in CI/CD for new policies?
- How would you handle exception paths during incidents without breaking policy?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests practical data processing in JavaScript: time-window filtering, per-user aggregation, and deterministic tie-breaking.

## Key Concepts
- Time-window filtering
- Hash map for counts
- Stable sorting with tie-breakers
- Input validation and edge cases

## Code Example

```javascript
function topUsers(events) {
  const now = Date.now();
  const window = 24 * 60 * 60 * 1000;
  const counts = new Map();
  for (const e of events) {
    const t = Date.parse(e.ts);
    if (Number.isNaN(t)) continue;
    if (now - t <= window) {
      counts.set(e.userId, (counts.get(e.userId) || 0) + 1);
    }
  }
  const arr = Array.from(counts.entries());
  arr.sort((a, b) => b[1] - a[1] || a[0].localeCompare(b[0]));
  return arr.slice(0, 3).map(([id]) => id);
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you adapt this for streaming data?
- How would you test with synthetic data to cover edge cases?
